  <!-- Payments' public face: the only thing another module is allowed to know
       about this one. Three requests, three responses, three closed status enums
       and an interface — no logic, no infrastructure, and deliberately no
       reference to Payments itself.

       Zero PackageReference items and zero ProjectReference items, both on
       purpose, exactly as the Catalog and Inventory contracts assemblies. A
       consumer takes a dependency on this assembly and nothing else, so
       referencing it cannot drag EF Core or Payments' entities into a module
       that has no business seeing them.

       019 argues why a flat module gets a contracts assembly at all. Here the
       argument is stronger than it was for Catalog: Payments is the module
       Soundcheck extracts first (004), so this seam is the one that will
       actually be spent, and Orders' checkout tests need a gateway that declines
       on demand — substitution really performed, not hypothetical. -->

</Project>
